1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,899
<font color="#FFFFFF">Retrieval-based language models sharpen their
predictions by pulling examples from a huge</font>

2
00:00:05,979 --> 00:00:07,869
<font color="#FFFFFF">external datastore at test time,</font>

3
00:00:07,949 --> 00:00:12,732
<font color="#FFFFFF">but that nearest-neighbor search is slow and runs
at almost every single token.</font>

4
00:00:12,812 --> 00:00:16,610
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper introduces RetoMaton, short for
retrieval automaton,</font>

5
00:00:16,690 --> 00:00:20,611
<font color="#FFFFFF">which builds a weighted finite automaton on top of
the datastore.</font>

6
00:00:20,691 --> 00:00:25,659
<font color="#FFFFFF">By saving pointers between consecutive entries and
clustering entries into states,</font>

7
00:00:25,739 --> 00:00:31,446
<font color="#FFFFFF">RetoMaton lets the model follow cheap automaton
transitions instead of searching from scratch.</font>

8
00:00:31,526 --> 00:00:37,478
<font color="#FFFFFF">The result: up to eighty-three percent fewer
nearest-neighbor searches with no loss in perplexity,</font>

9
00:00:37,558 --> 00:00:42,280
<font color="#FFFFFF">or up to one point eight five lower perplexity when
the search budget is kept.</font>

10
00:00:42,660 --> 00:00:48,323
<font color="#FFFFFF">Retrieval-based language models improve on standard
neural models by fetching nearest-neighbor</font>

11
00:00:48,403 --> 00:00:52,845
<font color="#FFFFFF">examples from an external datastore and blending
them into the prediction.</font>

12
00:00:52,925 --> 00:00:57,489
<font color="#FFFFFF">The catch is cost: that datastore search can fire
at every single time step,</font>

13
00:00:57,569 --> 00:01:00,849
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it is far slower than the model's own forward pass.</font>

14
00:01:00,929 --> 00:01:06,104
<font color="#FFFFFF">This frequent search is the single most critical
bottleneck that keeps these otherwise</font>

15
00:01:06,184 --> 00:01:08,670
<font color="#FFFFFF">powerful models out of practical settings.</font>

16
00:01:09,050 --> 00:01:13,073
<font color="#FFFFFF">The key observation is that a flat datastore throws
away structure.</font>

17
00:01:13,153 --> 00:01:15,277
<font color="#FFFFFF">If a retrieved entry was useful now,</font>

18
00:01:15,357 --> 00:01:19,809
<font color="#FFFFFF">the entry that follows it in the original text is
very likely useful next.</font>

19
00:01:19,889 --> 00:01:24,585
<font color="#FFFFFF">And entries whose key vectors are close tend to be
followed by the same token.</font>

20
00:01:24,665 --> 00:01:29,545
<font color="#FFFFFF">Existing approaches like Adaptive Retrieval simply
learn when to skip the search,</font>

21
00:01:29,625 --> 00:01:36,220
<font color="#FFFFFF">but when they skip they fall back entirely on the
base language model and discard the retrieval distribution,</font>

22
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:39,710
<font color="#FFFFFF">which hurts most in domains where the base model is weak.</font>

23
00:01:40,090 --> 00:01:44,138
<font color="#FFFFFF">RetoMaton makes two changes to the datastore.</font>

24
00:01:44,218 --> 00:01:48,265
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

25
00:01:48,345 --> 00:01:52,392
<font color="#FFFFFF">it saves a pointer from every entry to the entry
that came right after it in the text.</font>

26
00:01:52,472 --> 00:01:56,520
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, it clusters entries with similar key
vectors into states,</font>

27
00:01:56,600 --> 00:02:00,647
<font color="#FFFFFF">and those states share their outgoing pointers.</font>

28
00:02:00,727 --> 00:02:04,775
<font color="#FFFFFF">Together these turn the flat datastore into a
weighted finite automaton.</font>

29
00:02:04,855 --> 00:02:08,903
<font color="#FFFFFF">Building it is completely unsupervised, requires no
extra training data,</font>

30
00:02:08,982 --> 00:02:13,030
<font color="#FFFFFF">and works whether the automaton is constructed from
the model's own training corpus or from a brand-new domain.</font>

31
00:02:13,410 --> 00:02:17,983
<font color="#FFFFFF">RetoMaton stores each datastore entry as a triple
of key, value, and pointer,</font>

32
00:02:18,063 --> 00:02:22,213
<font color="#FFFFFF">where the pointer references the entry that
followed it in the corpus.</font>

33
00:02:22,293 --> 00:02:25,234
<font color="#FFFFFF">Entries with close keys are clustered into states,</font>

34
00:02:25,314 --> 00:02:28,436
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a state inherits all the pointers of its members.</font>

35
00:02:28,516 --> 00:02:35,385
<font color="#FFFFFF">At test time the model keeps a small set of active
states and traverses the automaton alongside the language model.</font>

36
00:02:35,465 --> 00:02:41,186
<font color="#FFFFFF">To move forward it just follows the pointers of
entries whose value matches the generated token,</font>

37
00:02:41,266 --> 00:02:42,757
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is essentially free.</font>

38
00:02:42,837 --> 00:02:48,316
<font color="#FFFFFF">A full nearest-neighbor search is only triggered
when the number of valid onward transitions</font>

39
00:02:48,396 --> 00:02:50,008
<font color="#FFFFFF">drops below a threshold tau.</font>

40
00:02:50,088 --> 00:02:55,144
<font color="#FFFFFF">The automaton's transition weights are computed
dynamically from the distance between</font>

41
00:02:55,224 --> 00:02:58,468
<font color="#FFFFFF">the current hidden state and the entries in each state,</font>

42
00:02:58,548 --> 00:03:03,060
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the resulting distribution is interpolated with
the base language model.</font>

43
00:03:03,440 --> 00:03:07,498
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method is evaluated in two settings.</font>

44
00:03:07,578 --> 00:03:11,636
<font color="#FFFFFF">For standard in-domain language modeling the
authors use WikiText-103,</font>

45
00:03:11,716 --> 00:03:15,774
<font color="#FFFFFF">a Wikipedia benchmark with one hundred and three
million training tokens,</font>

46
00:03:15,854 --> 00:03:19,912
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a two hundred forty seven million parameter
Transformer as the base model,</font>

47
00:03:19,992 --> 00:03:24,050
<font color="#FFFFFF">producing a datastore of one hundred and three
million entries clustered into one million states.</font>

48
00:03:24,130 --> 00:03:28,188
<font color="#FFFFFF">For domain adaptation they use the law-domain
corpus Law-MT with nineteen million tokens</font>

49
00:03:28,268 --> 00:03:32,326
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a larger six hundred fifty six million
parameter base model,</font>

50
00:03:32,406 --> 00:03:36,464
<font color="#FFFFFF">clustered into two hundred thousand states.</font>

51
00:03:36,544 --> 00:03:40,602
<font color="#FFFFFF">Throughout,</font>

52
00:03:40,682 --> 00:03:44,740
<font color="#FFFFFF">RetoMaton is compared against the original kNN-LM
and against Adaptive Retrieval.</font>

53
00:03:45,120 --> 00:03:49,184
<font color="#FFFFFF">The results are strong in both regimes.</font>

54
00:03:49,264 --> 00:03:53,327
<font color="#FFFFFF">On WikiText-103,</font>

55
00:03:53,407 --> 00:03:57,471
<font color="#FFFFFF">RetoMaton matches the perplexity of kNN-LM while
skipping eighty-one percent of the searches,</font>

56
00:03:57,551 --> 00:04:01,615
<font color="#FFFFFF">and even when it performs a search at every step it
still lowers perplexity,</font>

57
00:04:01,695 --> 00:04:05,758
<font color="#FFFFFF">because the carried-over pointers reinforce the
correct neighbors.</font>

58
00:04:05,838 --> 00:04:09,902
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the Law-MT domain-adaptation task the gains are
larger and much more robust: perplexity</font>

59
00:04:09,982 --> 00:04:14,045
<font color="#FFFFFF">drops from twelve point three four to ten point four nine,</font>

60
00:04:14,125 --> 00:04:18,189
<font color="#FFFFFF">and as more searches are saved RetoMaton's
perplexity climbs only very gently while plain</font>

61
00:04:18,269 --> 00:04:22,333
<font color="#FFFFFF">kNN-LM's perplexity blows up exponentially.</font>

62
00:04:22,413 --> 00:04:26,476
<font color="#FFFFFF">Overall the automaton either cuts perplexity by up
to one point eight five,</font>

63
00:04:26,556 --> 00:04:30,620
<font color="#FFFFFF">or saves up to eighty-three percent of the searches
with no loss.</font>

64
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,743
<font color="#FFFFFF">An ablation teases apart the two ingredients.</font>

65
00:04:33,823 --> 00:04:36,754
<font color="#FFFFFF">Using pointers alone, with no clustering at all,</font>

66
00:04:36,834 --> 00:04:42,838
<font color="#FFFFFF">already beats every baseline and matches kNN-LM
while saving more than sixty percent of searches,</font>

67
00:04:42,918 --> 00:04:47,354
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the pointers deliver most of the benefit when
few searches are saved.</font>

68
00:04:47,434 --> 00:04:50,553
<font color="#FFFFFF">Clustering contributes mainly at high saving rates,</font>

69
00:04:50,633 --> 00:04:52,686
<font color="#FFFFFF">from about seventy percent onward,</font>

70
00:04:52,766 --> 00:04:57,139
<font color="#FFFFFF">where it lets the model stay search-free over
longer stretches of text.</font>

71
00:04:57,219 --> 00:05:01,718
<font color="#FFFFFF">On cluster count, half a million and one million
means perform similarly,</font>

72
00:05:01,798 --> 00:05:04,290
<font color="#FFFFFF">while one hundred thousand is too coarse,</font>

73
00:05:04,370 --> 00:05:10,500
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a cheaper greedy clustering wins at zero saved
searches but fades as the saving fraction grows.</font>

74
00:05:10,880 --> 00:05:13,618
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline numbers are simple to remember.</font>

75
00:05:13,698 --> 00:05:19,957
<font color="#FFFFFF">RetoMaton saves up to eighty-three percent of
nearest-neighbor searches with no loss in perplexity,</font>

76
00:05:20,037 --> 00:05:26,425
<font color="#FFFFFF">or alternatively lowers perplexity by as much as
one point eight five when the search budget is kept.</font>

77
00:05:26,505 --> 00:05:31,548
<font color="#FFFFFF">On WikiText-103 it matches kNN-LM while skipping
eighty-one percent of searches.</font>

78
00:05:31,628 --> 00:05:38,720
<font color="#FFFFFF">And on a fine-tuned law-domain model it pushes
perplexity from eight point six one down to seven point one zero,</font>

79
00:05:38,800 --> 00:05:42,050
<font color="#FFFFFF">a relative reduction of more than seventeen percent.</font>

80
00:05:42,430 --> 00:05:47,254
<font color="#FFFFFF">The lasting idea is that a retrieval datastore has
structure worth exploiting.</font>

81
00:05:47,334 --> 00:05:53,100
<font color="#FFFFFF">By linking consecutive entries with pointers and
grouping similar ones into automaton states,</font>

82
00:05:53,180 --> 00:06:00,204
<font color="#FFFFFF">RetoMaton lets a language model carry retrieval
forward in time instead of searching from scratch at every token.</font>

83
00:06:00,284 --> 00:06:04,730
<font color="#FFFFFF">It is unsupervised, works with any base model,
transfers across domains,</font>

84
00:06:04,810 --> 00:06:07,811
<font color="#FFFFFF">and unifies token, chunk, and sequence retrieval,</font>

85
00:06:07,891 --> 00:06:12,400
<font color="#FFFFFF">all while cutting the dominant cost of
retrieval-based language modeling.</font>
